{"title":"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A given convex pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13a96240c51d61ba3733d9f5c3020fa92ec4136.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" /> has the property that the area of each of the five triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7ada763e99852a6e44a15d46cce3c3709a944d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/b/c7bc69328ef74607767b467b2a851f202438edec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CDE$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/1/291a26605884e355c23c524c392c07ad49e5df5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DEA$\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e30e7dec97a3f5e84e0de8a12e3056c6c9a77fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EAB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> is unity. Show that all pentagons with the above property have the same area, and calculate that area. Show, furthermore, that there are infinitely many non-congruent pentagons having the above area property.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/4/cf420a20a2d4376c893713c2b0387cecb4bb347b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(120); defaultpen(fontsize(10)); pair A=dir(90), B=dir(90-72), C=dir(90-2*72), D=dir(90-3*72), E=dir(90-4*72); draw(A--B--C--D--E--cycle); draw(A--C--E--B--D--cycle); label(&quot;A&quot;,A,A);label(&quot;B&quot;,B,B);label(&quot;C&quot;,C,C);label(&quot;D&quot;,D,D);label(&quot;E&quot;,E,E); [/asy]\" width=\"202\" height=\"192\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Lemma:</b> Convex pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299d6f59a4eef8cdda087b433cc21f8789675ba4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0A_1A_2A_3A_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" /> has the property that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/2/452dc6ae90570150fa1818eabec795e7d770ec5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[A_0A_1A_2] = [A_1A_2A_3] = [A_2A_3A_4] = [A_3A_4A_0] = [A_4A_0A_1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"461\" height=\"18\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d8bff21915c1657fb44829d16ba4db18616c284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{A_{n - 1}A_{n + 1}}\\parallel\\overline{A_{n - 2}A_{n + 2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"178\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/8/5b83017a93e0fab9da436e15005d83e8935fb652.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" /> (indices taken mod 5).\n</p><p><b>Proof:</b> For the \"only if\" direction, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/0/700a2868cd522d4a911dfda540adde12a3677ea6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[A_0A_1A_2] = [A_1A_2A_3]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"169\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/c/7cce761941ab4b57ae903d05b8fa0568ebbdd98f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791fe65c9d1ed7d8ed33a5025f40c5c7f9e476af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> are equidistant from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/4/df499b421b4c85e0ed8ded9c2314c922d2485c2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{A_1A_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" />, and since the pentagon is convex, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/e/9de28d25c8d8d2520b28d74e7e6277ad93ce8666.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{A_0A_3}\\parallel\\overline{A_1A_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />. The other four pairs of parallel lines are established by a symmetrical argument, and the proof for the other direction is just this, but reversed.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d803ab47dfdec59681471fd43a3051208a7a10fe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(120); defaultpen(fontsize(10)); pathpen = black; pair A=MP(&quot;A&quot;,dir(90),dir(90)), B=MP(&quot;B&quot;,dir(90-72),dir(90-72)), C=MP(&quot;C&quot;,dir(90-2*72),dir(90-2*72)), D=MP(&quot;D&quot;,dir(90-3*72),dir(90-3*72)), E=MP(&quot;E&quot;,dir(90-4*72),dir(90-4*72)); D(A--B--C--D--E--cycle); D(A--C--E--B--D--cycle); pair Ap = MP(&quot;A&#39;&quot;,IP(B--D,C--E),dir(270)), Bp = MP(&quot;B&#39;&quot;,IP(A--D,C--E),dir(270-72)), Cp = MP(&quot;C&#39;&quot;,IP(A--D,B--E),dir(270-72*2)), Dp = MP(&quot;D&#39;&quot;,IP(A--C,B--E),dir(270-72*3)), Ep = MP(&quot;E&#39;&quot;,IP(A--C,B--D),dir(270-72*4)); [/asy]\" width=\"202\" height=\"188\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/1/cf159bb3cc1e14c2700b26ebd444341bc566b111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;D&#39;E&#39;$\" width=\"95\" height=\"14\" /> be the inner pentagon, labeled so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" /> are opposite each other, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/c/00cbe98d2864aae78c25b55b49ae5482c8a50e2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d, e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> be the side lengths of the inner pentagon labeled opposite their corresponding vertices. From the lemma, pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/1/cf159bb3cc1e14c2700b26ebd444341bc566b111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;D&#39;E&#39;$\" width=\"95\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13a96240c51d61ba3733d9f5c3020fa92ec4136.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b5029bd44b251390aae28f4b9fb2d0605978b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = m(A&#39;B&#39;)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"20\" /> and parallelogram <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/e/46e2d16fbaf7a053494430cb20b677ff467164c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCB&#39;$\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> (and cyclic) has area 2. Supposing\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/a/afaf87a97b8ff9e632267ef6bbe2af73d2e18b21.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} P &amp;= [ABCDE] \\\\ Q &amp;= [A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;D&#39;E&#39;] \\\\ R &amp;= \\sum_{\\text{cyc}}[AC&#39;D&#39;] \\\\ S &amp;= \\sum_{\\text{cyc}}[ABD&#39;], \\end{align*}\" width=\"142\" height=\"144\" />\nwe have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fcc671ba254e003fe6bfb5860c1c9b743781a139.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \\sum_{\\text{cyc}}[ABCB&#39;] &amp;= 5Q + 3R + 2S \\\\ 10 &amp;= 3Q + R + 2P. \\end{align*}\" width=\"233\" height=\"65\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/e/caed5cf9ff3e381b68525e8a3ba605cd919976c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCDC&#39;$\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/3/783050d92b823823883af25aa3f94794b12138d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CDED&#39;$\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" /> are parallelograms, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/8/a880ac33950c981a25707603e5723b29973dcf75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD&#39; = EC&#39; = (m - 1)a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"190\" height=\"20\" />. Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/c/decedbd7ef9e158a59debf72cce913a54405df3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EB&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/b/ecb4cd2a13596695c67bb16257a68f9cd006073d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ECB$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/3/493c2599ee0acb9d27eff077520be0aa6b589e6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$me = BC = \\left(\\frac{2m - 1}{m - 1}\\right)e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"199\" height=\"43\" />, and with the requirement that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/c/1cce1bd621aff652b0983cbc94a24545e5f4bdcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/b/45bac4e653b91ed496e46e1444072aa6f8878d15.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m = \\frac{2m - 1}{m - 1}\\Rightarrow m = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"41\" />\nNow, we compute that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/3/63366446f3a3d26d249031ddb8fe9c2c75206962.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[AC&#39;D&#39;] = \\frac{1}{2m - 1} = \\sqrt{5} - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"226\" height=\"37\" />, and similar computation for the other four triangles gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/0/db0446ba01e10b6e528b13db36dc90d710589383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R = 5\\sqrt{5} - 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />. From the aforementioned pentagon similarity, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/990ad19557667f0e132d31979fe0cd92ba5dabc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = P/m^2 = \\left(\\frac{7 - 3\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"222\" height=\"53\" />. Solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d73543b563120adedd1f2ce93818eee80106c9a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} 10 &amp;= 3\\left(\\frac{7 - 3\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)P + (5\\sqrt{5} - 10) + 2P \\\\ 20 - 5\\sqrt{5} &amp;= \\left(\\frac{25 - 9\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)P \\\\ P &amp;= \\frac{40 - 10\\sqrt{5}}{25 - 9\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ &amp;= \\frac{5 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"379\" height=\"214\" />\n</p><p>To show that there are infinitely many pentagons with the given property, we start with triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/2/022948689ac03ea1705a6af78b86d32ae2a369eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> and construct the original pentagon using the required length ratios. This triangle has three continuous degrees of freedom, and the condition that the equal areas be specifically unity means that there are two continuous degrees of freedom in which the configuration can vary, which implies the desired result.\n</p><p>Alternatively, one can shear a regular pentagon, a transformation which preserves areas and the property of a pair of lines being parallel.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO\" title=\"1972 USAMO\">1972 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1972\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1972 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017171116\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.036 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.062 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 244/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1237/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8748/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   22.516      1 -total\n 73.98%   16.658      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 24.71%    5.563      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7527:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017171116 and revision id 191365. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}